        opts: [
            "The mill, because late delivery of raw materials is never a material breach under the Uniform Commercial Code.",
            "The manufacturer, because under UCC § 2-601, any failure to make timely delivery is a breach entitling the buyer to cancel.",
            "The manufacturer, because the contract was automatically voided on the thirty-first day by operation of commercial trade usage.",
            "The mill, because the manufacturer failed to issue a written demand for adequate assurance before canceling."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: Under the UCC Perfect Tender Rule (UCC § 2-601), if the goods or the tender of delivery fail in any respect to conform to the contract, the buyer may reject the whole or cancel the contract. Conformance includes timeliness of delivery. When a contract is silent as to time, UCC § 2-309(1) implies a reasonable time, which is construed in light of trade usage (UCC § 1-303). Because trade usage established a 30-day delivery expectation, the mill's failure to tender goods within 35 days without excuse constituted a failure of tender, entitling the manufacturer to treat the delayed tender as a breach and cancel the contract (Option B). Option A is incorrect because the perfect tender rule applies to timing of delivery as well as physical specifications. Option C is incorrect because delay makes a contract voidable by the aggrieved party's election, not automatically void. Option D is incorrect because the manufacturer did not have mere insecurity about future performance; the time for performance had already expired without delivery, constituting an actual breach rather than an anticipatory insecurity."
